(footprint "Relay_DPDT_Finder-40.52"
	(version 20231120)
	(generator "temper-hand")
	(generator_version "8.0")
	(layer "F.Cu")
	(descr "Finder 40.52 series, 2 CO (DPDT) miniature PCB power relay, standard DC sensitive coil, 12VDC (coil code 7.012 per Finder '40 SERIES PCB/Plug-in relays 8-10-12-16A' catalog, cat. no. S40EN, https://cdn.findernet.com/app/uploads/S40EN.pdf): full orderable part number 40.52.7.012.0000 (unchanged from the 2026-07-28 relay-replacement pass -- see docs/evidence/2026-07-28-relay-replacement-implementation.md for the part-selection rationale, not re-litigated here). PD3 RE-TARGET (2026-07-28, docs/evidence/2026-07-28-pd3-retarget-relay.md): the prior footprint's pin coordinates (11mm coil-to-contact center-to-center, 1.0mm drill) were a SELF-ACKNOWLEDGED invented layout, not read from the manufacturer's own PCB pattern (that session's own descr said so explicitly: 'not a trace of the manufacturer's own recommended PCB pattern'). THIS footprint replaces that invented geometry with the REAL manufacturer dimensions, measured this session via pixel-calibrated analysis of the catalog's own p.3 'Copper side view' technical drawing for type 40.52 (dimension text is vector-drawn, not present in the PDF's extractable text layer -- confirmed via pdftotext, consistent with every prior session's extraction difficulty with this exact drawing): case envelope 29 x 12.4mm (independently corroborated by the catalog's p.10 outline drawing, exact match); pin diameter 1.5mm (drawing callout, circled-diameter symbol); contact pitch 5.0mm (three contacts per pole, evenly spaced, matches the catalog's own stated 'FEATURES: 5.0mm pin pitch' bullet); row separation (coil row to contact row) 7.5mm (matches this footprint's pre-existing, correct assumption, unchanged); and, the load-bearing correction, COIL-PIN-TO-NEAREST-CONTACT-PIN SPACING = 7.5mm CENTER-TO-CENTER, not 11mm. Measurement method: two independent dimension chains in the drawing (box-left-edge-to-coil-pin = 2.5mm, and box-left-edge-to-farthest-contact-pin = 20mm, both referenced to the same 29mm case-width datum) were cross-validated by pixel-position calibration against the drawing's own labelled 29mm case width and 5mm contact pitch; both chains agree to within 0.5mm (measurement noise), giving coil-to-nearest-contact = 10mm(box-edge-to-first-contact, i.e. 20mm minus two 5mm pitches) minus 2.5mm(box-edge-to-coil) = 7.5mm, confirmed directly by pixel measurement of the coil-dot-to-first-contact-dot separation (7.36-7.41mm raw pixel read, consistent with the clean 7.5mm derived from the labelled dimensions within calibration error). SECOND CORRECTION, also newly found this session: the manufacturer's own drawing places the pin order, left-to-right (nearest-coil to farthest), as NC-COM-NO (e.g. pole 1: pin '12'=NC nearest the coil, pin '11'=COM in the middle, pin '14'=NO farthest) -- NOT COM-NC-NO as the prior footprint (and components.ato's own docstring, unchanged there since it only describes pin-to-function identity, not physical order) assumed. This is a physical-position correction only; it does not change the Relay_DPDT component's pin<->signal mapping in elec/src/components.ato (COM1~pin\"11\", NC1~pin\"12\", NO1~pin\"14\" remain correct identity assignments -- what was wrong was WHERE pad \"11\" and pad \"12\" sat, not what they are named). Pad sizing corrected to fit the real 1.5mm pin: 1.7mm drill (1.5mm pin + 0.2mm clearance, standard THT practice), 2.2mm pad diameter (0.25mm annular ring) -- the prior footprint's 1.0mm drill could not physically accept this part's real 1.5mm pins at all, a second, independent defect now also fixed. RESULT: with the corrected, real geometry, the governing (shortest) coil<->contact edge-to-edge creepage is 5.300mm (A1<->pin 12, or A2<->pin 22 by symmetry) -- independently re-verified this session by parsing this saved file directly (not asserted from design intent), script and result in docs/evidence/2026-07-28-pd3-retarget-relay.md. 5.300mm FAILS both the PD3 12.6mm target (by 7.300mm) and the prior PD2-era 8.0mm target (by 2.700mm). NO ROUTED SLOT IS ADDED to try to close this gap: this relay's own one-piece 29x12.4mm moulded case body (F.Fab/F.CrtYd outline below) sits directly over the entire coil-to-contact pin field with no internal gap or cutout (confirmed against the catalog's own outline drawing, p.10, which shows a single continuous case body, no slot or window feature) -- the identical failure mode this project's own docs/evidence/2026-07-28-creepage-determination-brainstorm.md Section 7 already established for the incumbent Omron G5LE-1 ('the relay body sits on the board; a slot cannot lengthen a path that runs over the part's own case'). A PCB groove cut in this region would sit entirely beneath the relay's own base and provide no creepage benefit (per docs/evidence/2026-07-28-isolator-creepage-slots.md Section 3's own geometric derivation: an enclosed slot's benefit comes from forcing a detour around its END on an EXPOSED top surface, not from board thickness -- and there is no exposed top surface here for a groove to lengthen, since the relay's case already bridges directly across it at the same short distance). See the evidence doc for the full determination, the PD2/PD3 sensitivity analysis, and the required next steps (this is a part-selection or board-level finding, not a footprint-design gap -- the creepage target was NOT reduced to fit this part). Terminal numbering: EN 50005 standard relay convention (A1/A2=coil; pole 1: 11=COM/12=NC/14=NO; pole 2: 21=COM/22=NC/24=NO), unchanged identity assignments from the prior session, now placed at their real physical positions. VERIFY the manufacturer's actual CAD/STEP file (downloadable from findernet.com) against this hand-measured layout before fabrication -- this session's measurement is a rigorous pixel-calibrated read of the catalog's own vector drawing, cross-validated two independent ways, but is still not a substitute for the vendor's own CAD export.")
	(tags "relay DPDT Finder 40.52 power relay reinforced PD3-retarget")
